WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.087
Module 4 focused heavily on aggregation.

00:00:03.087 --> 00:00:08.662
We asked questions such as: how many admissions occurred, what is the average bill amount,

00:00:08.662 --> 00:00:11.807
or how many records belong to each category?

00:00:11.807 --> 00:00:18.272
Module 5 asks a different kind of question: which rule applies to each row?

00:00:18.272 --> 00:00:22.307
Suppose a company is building a customer-retention campaign.

00:00:22.307 --> 00:00:28.512
The business rule might say: include customers who are active, who have agreed to receive messages,

00:00:28.512 --> 00:00:33.832
and who either have not purchased in ninety days or have recently submitted a complaint.

00:00:33.832 --> 00:00:36.942
Notice how quickly the logic becomes layered.

00:00:36.942 --> 00:00:41.897
We have AND connecting required conditions, OR connecting alternatives,

00:00:41.897 --> 00:00:47.192
and possibly missing values for consent, purchase dates, or complaint status.

00:00:47.192 --> 00:00:49.677
The same thing happens in healthcare.

00:00:49.677 --> 00:00:56.052
A patient may qualify for follow-up only when the patient is active, consent is explicitly true,

00:00:56.052 --> 00:01:01.367
the diagnosis belongs to a target group, and there is no usable email address.

00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:06.577
If consent is NULL, should that be treated as false?

00:01:06.577 --> 00:01:11.737
Maybe not. It may mean consent has never been recorded, which could require manual review.

00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:17.282
This is why SQL logic should start from the policy, not from the syntax.

00:01:17.282 --> 00:01:22.517
We will repeatedly use the same process: write the rule in plain English,

00:01:22.517 --> 00:01:27.477
identify fields that may be missing, group related conditions clearly, test boundary cases,

00:01:27.477 --> 00:01:32.177
and then compare the result with the original rule.

00:01:32.177 --> 00:01:37.372
A query can be perfectly valid SQL and still be analytically wrong.

00:01:37.372 --> 00:01:42.294
In this module, we are training ourselves to catch those quiet logic errors.
